**Core Data Requirements (MUST HAVE)**

**1. Enhanced Historical Actuals**

Beyond just weekly net cash flow, request:

Weekly Actuals Breakdown:

\- Operating cash inflows (by category)

\* Customer payments (by segment: automotive, construction, industrial)

\* Other operating income

\- Operating cash outflows (by category)

\* Raw material purchases

\* Payroll & benefits

\* Energy costs

\* Supplier payments

\* Operating expenses

\- Financing activities

\* Debt payments/drawdowns

\* Interest payments

\- Investing activities

\* Capex payments

\* Asset sales

\- Tax payments

\- Dividend payments

**Why?** Category-level patterns are more predictable than aggregate
totals.

**2. Forward-Looking Internal Data**

Sales & Production:

\- Order book (confirmed orders, delivery dates)

\- Production schedule (next 8 weeks)

\- Sales pipeline (expected contract signings)

Payables/Receivables:

\- Accounts receivable aging (what\'s due when)

\- Accounts payable schedule (what you owe when)

\- Average collection period by customer segment

\- Payment terms by customer/supplier

Known Future Events:

\- Scheduled debt service dates

\- Tax payment dates

\- Planned capex expenditures

\- Dividend payment dates

\- Payroll schedule

**Why?** These create strong leading indicators for cash movements.

**External Data (HIGH VALUE)**

**3. Industry & Market Indicators**

Steel Industry Specific:

\- Steel price indices (HRC, CRC prices)

\- Raw material prices (iron ore, scrap, alloys)

\- Industry capacity utilization

\- Construction activity indices (major markets)

\- Automotive production forecasts

Regional Economic:

\- Manufacturing PMI (Europe)

\- Construction spending indices

\- Industrial production indices

**Why?** These predict demand changes and pricing power, affecting
customer payment timing and amounts.

**4. FX Rates**

Key Currency Pairs:

\- EUR/USD (if selling to US)

\- EUR/GBP (UK exposure)

\- EUR/CNY (China raw materials)

\- Other relevant pairs based on customer/supplier locations

Request:

\- Daily spot rates (aggregate to weekly)

\- Forward rates if they hedge

\- Exposure by currency (what % of inflows/outflows in each currency)

**Why?** FX movements directly impact cash flows for multinational
operations.

**5. Interest Rates**

Relevant Rates:

\- EURIBOR (if debt is floating rate)

\- ECB policy rate

\- Credit spreads

\- Their actual borrowing rates

Request:

\- Debt structure (fixed vs floating, maturity schedule)

\- Interest rate exposure

**Why?** Impacts financing costs and investment returns on excess cash.

**Macroeconomic Indicators (MEDIUM VALUE)**

**6. Economic Conditions**

Leading Indicators:

\- GDP growth forecasts (key markets)

\- Consumer confidence

\- Business sentiment indices

\- Unemployment rates

Financial Markets:

\- Stock market indices (correlate with business confidence)

\- Credit spreads (liquidity conditions)

\- Commodity indices

**Why?** Broad economic conditions affect customer payment behavior and
demand.

**Operational Data (USEFUL FOR REFINEMENT)**

**7. Customer Behavior Data**

Customer Analytics:

\- Top 20 customers (payment history, patterns)

\- Customer payment reliability scores

\- Seasonal payment patterns by customer segment

\- Days sales outstanding (DSO) trends

**Why?** Large customers drive most cash volatility. Understanding their
patterns improves forecast accuracy.

**8. Supplier Dynamics**

Supplier Information:

\- Top suppliers (payment terms, critical suppliers)

\- Early payment discount opportunities

\- Payment timing flexibility

**Why?** Outflow timing can sometimes be managed/optimized.

**Seasonal & Calendar Effects**

**9. Calendar Data**

Important Dates:

\- Working days per week

\- Bank holidays (multiple countries)

\- Month-end/quarter-end flags

\- Typical customer payment days (30th, 15th, etc.)

\- Industry shutdown periods (summer, year-end)

**Why?** Cash flows are heavily influenced by calendar effects.

